\details{
\strong{Method}

With \eqn{P} the table divided by its grand total, and \eqn{r} and
\eqn{c} its row and column sums (the sample and feature weights),
correspondence analysis (Legendre & Legendre 2012, §9.2; Greenacre 2007)
finds the principal components of the matrix of chi-square residuals
\eqn{\bar{Q} = D_r^{-1/2} (P - rc^T) D_c^{-1/2}}. With
\eqn{\bar{Q} = U \Sigma V^T}, the eigenvalues are the squared singular
values, the site scores (scaling 2) are \eqn{D_r^{-1/2} U}, and the
species scores are \eqn{D_c^{-1/2} V \Sigma}.

The axes come from block Lanczos iteration, which needs only products of
\eqn{\bar{Q}} with a few vectors at a time. \eqn{\bar{Q}} is a sparse
matrix less a rank-one term, so these are computed in parallel from the
table's non-zero values alone, and a sparse table is never made dense.
\code{ca()} shares this solver with \code{\link[=cca]{cca()}}: \code{ca(counts)} gives the same
scores, eigenvalues and loadings as \code{cca(counts)} with no constraints.

\strong{CA or PCA?}

Correspondence analysis assumes that each species responds unimodally to
the underlying gradients, peaking at some optimum; PCA assumes linear
responses. Over long gradients, where many species are absent from much
of the range, CA suits better; over short ones, PCA of transformed
abundances (\code{pca(counts, norm = 'hellinger')}) is often preferred
(Legendre & Gallagher 2001). The chi-square metric gives rare species a
large weight, so CA can be sensitive to them. CA axes of long gradients
can also show an "arch" (the second axis a curved function of the
first).

\strong{Matching other software}

\code{ca()} gives the same site scores, eigenvalues, inertia and species
scores as \code{vegan::ca(counts)} and \code{vegan::cca(counts)} (scaling 2), and as
\code{skbio.stats.ordination.ca()} in scaling 2, up to the signs of the axes.
\code{ade4::dudi.coa()} gives the same eigenvalues; its \verb{$l1} (row scores of
unit weighted variance) are \code{ca()}'s scores, and its \verb{$co} (column
principal coordinates) are \code{ca()}'s loadings. Samples with no observations are
an error, as in \code{vegan} and \code{ade4}; features with no observations have
no weight, and get loadings of 0.

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{vegan::ca()} and \code{ca::ca()} share their name with ecodive's \code{ca()}, and
whichever package was attached last masks the other's. Write
\code{ecodive::ca()}, or use \code{\link[=count_ord]{count_ord()}}, which always runs ecodive's, to be
sure. \code{ade4::dudi.coa()} computes the same analysis under another name.

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

An eigenvector's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return
mirror images of the same axis. \code{ca()} flips each axis so that its
largest score in absolute value is positive, and flips the loadings with
it. Results are identical from run to run and for any number of \code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them. The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
